WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.375 --> 00:00:08.080
- My name is Tracy Rabin.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.320
And I'm Director of the
Office of Global Health

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.080
in the Department of Internal Medicine

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.200
at the Yale School of Medicine.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.600
I'm also the co-director of the

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.840
YIGH Global Health Ethics Program.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.880
And it is truly an honor today to be able

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.800
to introduce you to the featured speaker

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.360
for our very special YIGH
event Dr. Olusoji Adeyi.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.000
Dr. Adeyi's career accomplishments
are truly impressive.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.040
Before I share a bit of his background.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.200
I wanna summarize by saying that if

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.320
the true measure of our successes are the

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.240
number of lives that have been changed for

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.400
the better because of one's efforts,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.080
both direct and indirect,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.320
then he is among our times most eminent.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.920
Dr. Adeyi is currently the President

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.520
of Resilient Health Systems,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.640
a Washington DC based Policy Analysis

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.680
and Advisory Services Firm.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.600
And he's also a Senior Associate at

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.840
the Johns Hopkins Bloomberg
School of Public Health.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.760
Over the course of his
more than 30 year career,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.440
focusing on global health policy,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.160
he has held leadership positions with

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.280
the World Bank,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.960
World Health Organization,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.080
UNAIDS, the Nigerian
Federal Ministry of Health.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.520
And was the founding director of

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.320
the Affordable Medicines Facility Malaria,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.640
a public private partnership posted by

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.480
the Global Fund to fight AIDS,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.160
tuberculosis and malaria.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.200
With a focus on improving the structure

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.800
and function of health systems,

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.960
these positions have allowed him truly to

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.640
touch every region of the world.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.880
Born in Nigeria, Dr. Adeyi earned his

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.560
Bachelor's in Medical Degrees from the

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.440
University of Ife and
subsequently received

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.360
a Master of Community Health from the

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.120
Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.680
a Master of Business Administration

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.320
from Imperial College
London, University of London.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.600
And ultimately his Doctor
of Public Health from

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.960
the Johns Hopkins University.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.840
When we spoke a few weeks ago,

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.680
he shared that it was his early work

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.840
as a physician that motivated him to

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.720
pursue additional training and to set off

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.080
on this very unique journey.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.320
In that role he had
the personal experience

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.320
of seeing the devastating impact that

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.960
financial policies influenced by national

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.040
priorities and international donors

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.960
had on patients and the health system.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.320
And as he told me I
wanted to be in a position

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.160
to influence policy rather than

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.360
being on the receiving end.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.240
Along those lines he had also shared

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:33.840
the perspective that it's important for

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.480
those of us who seek global health careers

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.320
to embark on this journey for reasons

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.320
that are personally meaningful.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.800
As this is what will stiffen the spine

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.320
and keep us moving forward in

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.600
the face of adversity.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.480
I am certain that this sense of purpose

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.720
will come through in today's conversation.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.400
And I look forward to the other lessons

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.520
that he has to share with us.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.720
Dr. Adeyi, thank you so much

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.000
for joining us here today.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.160
I'll turn the podium over now to

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.640
the moderator for today's Q&amp;A session.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.720
My Yale School of Public Health colleague,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.000
Raphael Perez-Escamilla,
Professor of Public Health

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.320
and Director of the Office
of Public Health Practice

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.240
and the Global Health Concentration.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.840
Looking forward to a
very robust conversation.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.480
- Thank you so very much, Tracey

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.080
for your kind introduction and Olusoji,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.600
what an honor to be able to serve as a

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.720
host today for you to
share with our audience

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.120
why did you decide to take the somewhat

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:42.080
painful step of writing a book to actually

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.520
share your experiences in global health?

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.280
What is the niche that
you tried to position

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.480
your book when you decided to write it?

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.560
- Thank you very much, Tracey for your

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.880
kind introduction and
Raphael and your team

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.280
for this opportunity to
have this conversation.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:05.520
You refer to the process of

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.040
writing the book as painful.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:07.680
That's true.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.280
I think there were two
other dimensions to it.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.440
One is it was actually a joy

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.840
because it was so
instructive writing the book.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.200
And this is the book I
wish I had when I was 25.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.520
And I've written it
several times in my mind,

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.280
but I finally had an
opportunity to put it on paper.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.960
So what is the motivation behind the book?

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.600
The ultimate motivation is for those of us

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.160
who are engaged in public health,

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.400
whether as policy makers,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.000
as analysts,

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.160
as physicians

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.040
or influencers,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.040
that we step back reexamine the roots

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.840
of global health take stock of

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.360
what has worked well

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.440
and importantly, honestly, appraise why

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.200
it is so unhappy today,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.040
why global health is so unhappy.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.200
But that we do so not because we want to

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.840
then slump into negativity

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.600
that nothing can ever improve.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.000
Also we do so with a
renewed sense of purpose

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:23.440
and clear eyed optimism that

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.720
we can make it better.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.320
That is the ultimate purpose of the book.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.920
And which is why my favorite sentence

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.240
in the book is actually the

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.520
last sentence in the book.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.000
Which says, "A brighter
future is possible."

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.160
- Thank you very much Olusoji.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.760
And I do want to share with you

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:48.080
and the audience that I worked

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.320
for three years in your beautiful

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:55.040
country of Nigeria in the State of Kaduna

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.240
with maternal infant

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.880
and young child feeding project.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.480
And through that project I learned

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.160
on the one hand how complex Nigeria was

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:16.000
and is from political economic systems,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.080
healthcare systems, the
patient systems point of view.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.280
But at the same time I also learned about

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.960
the incredibly rich culture

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:28.160
and traditions

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.480
and quite frankly
experiences that the country

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.280
has had over many, many
decades now trying to

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.280
improve a number of healthcare outcomes

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.600
that are not only unique to Nigeria,

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:52.640
but also to the rest of the world.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.280
So can you tell us a little bit about how

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:59.120
having been born raised in Nigeria

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.080
actually ended up informing

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.040
your decision to end up working in

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.280
the field of global health

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.480
to do it from a capacity
building perspective?

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:15.200
And lastly, what have you learned from

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:17.520
your own experience with regards to the

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.360
legacy of colonialism and neocolonialism

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.440
on current global health inequities.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.640
- Thanks Rafael and now that I know

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.040
you are my fellow Nigerian,

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.760
I think one of these days you and I should

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.800
get together and share
a glass of palm wine,

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:37.840
which I'm sure you had when

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:38.720
you were in the country.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.120
But on the topic at hand in hindsight

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.960
it's all very clear now in hindsight.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.960
Much of what I do is influenced by

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.440
my own early experiences

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.720
and some form the
formative years in Nigeria,

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:56.400
excuse me.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.200
My mother is a retired business woman.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.360
My father was a nurse.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:08.000
So I saw quite a few cases
that he treated early on.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:09.760
And there's one that sticks in my mind.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.560
He was extracting Guinea
worm from somebody's leg.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.520
And that was quite impressive for a

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.840
five or six year old to witness.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.560
But in the larger social context,

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.880
I grew up in a society with its

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.640
own very powerful narrative.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.080
I was born in Oyo which is the capital of

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.840
an old empire that predated many of

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:38.240
the countries we're talking about.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.800
It predated the United
States, for example.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.280
And Oyo is called the land of
Shango, the God of thunder.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.200
We had a very strong sense

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.360
of our own social identity.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.840
I also did my undergraduate
years in Nigeria.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.640
And I went to medical school there too.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.880
And what I experienced there was

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.640
the part of an of education

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.640
and the stresses that poverty can put on

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.720
those who cannot afford the

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:12.000
healthcare that they need.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.040
I think that was a very searing experience

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.200
to witness as a young physician

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.240
when I witnessed that,

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.040
but also come from a background in which

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.800
we were taught from day one,

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.640
that you have to do something about

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:27.920
the world around you.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.840
You're not just gonna complain about it.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:33.600
So I opted to go into public health policy

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.840
as a result of that.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.280
Well, when I look back now,

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.640
I think two or three
things stand out to me,

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:48.080
the importance of a sense of purpose.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.360
This is for positive reasons

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:52.880
because it keeps us going.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:55.440
It keeps us going on a day to day basis.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.960
It also makes us more resilient to

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.040
the various ups and
downs that we experience

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.040
in the cause of the
career in global health.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:11.120
The second one is to
pay extra attention to

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:13.840
the realities of people with whom we work

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.080
in low and lower middle income countries.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:18.880
They have their own narratives.

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:21.920
They may not have studied epidemiology

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.120
and biostatistics

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:24.880
and health economics.

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:27.840
And so some of the things they do

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.160
and some of the ways they go about things

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.560
may not make sense to somebody

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.720
who has just parachuted in

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:35.840
from the global north.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:41.600
But if one has a certain
degree of humility

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:43.280
and a willingness to learn,

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.920
then it becomes possible to

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:47.440
understand their reality.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:50.480
It becomes possible to engage with them

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.400
in a very effective way.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:55.760
And it becomes possible for the

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.200
programs on which we work,

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:59.440
the analysis that we do to actually

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.040
have a multiplier effect.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.080
- Thank you, Olusoji.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:07.280
And as part of the subtitle of your book

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:11.200
you list investing amid pandemics

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.320
denial of evidence and neo dependency.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.200
Can you talk to us a little bit about

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.240
what do you mean by denial of evidence

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.000
as well as neo dependency

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.400
and why are those important to

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:26.440
understand and address?

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.320
- Thank you, Rafael.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:32.400
In our profession,

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.280
in public health policy

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:40.560
one of the tenants is to rigorously base

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:42.800
our recommendations and our programs

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:46.400
on scientific analysis.

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:51.600
Of course you adapt
from country to country

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.480
and even within countries so as to be

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.840
viable during implementation.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:11:59.280
And I think this is great.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.280
It's central to what we do it's central

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.120
to our professional credibility.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:06.880
However, if you look at the world

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:08.400
around us in global health,

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:13.040
that premise does not always hold up.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:15.040
So let me answer your question directly.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.600
One of the things about which I've been

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:19.920
most impressed in my career is the extent

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.600
to which evidence is swept aside

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:27.760
when it does not meet the preferences

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:30.000
of powerful outdoors in global health.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.560
There are many examples of those.

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.640
And I refer to a few in the book,

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:38.400
but the one that is really central

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:41.120
to the illustration in the book

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:43.360
is that of the United States Agency

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:45.920
for International Development, USAID

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:51.840
and the US President's
Malaria Initiative, USPMI.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:56.400
This is concerning access
to malaria medicines

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.320
for people in low income countries

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:05.840
despite rigorous evaluation
of a pilot program.

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:09.440
That in fact the approach
taken was working

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:15.360
USAID and USPMI did everything they could

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.040
to derail that program.

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:20.160
They bullied any entity that did not

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:21.680
subscribe to their point of view.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.040
And they successfully
bullied the global fund

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.320
to fight AIDS, TB and malaria into

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:29.840
discontinuing that program.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:34.000
Now, I'm not telling you any State secret.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:35.760
It just happens that I left that

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.320
enterprise so I had a front row seat

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:39.200
into what happened.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.400
So this is an instance in which

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:49.840
USAID and USPMI simply obliterated a

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:53.600
program that was
demonstratively on the path

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:55.680
to saving many lives in low

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:57.200
and lower middle income countries,

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:00.320
because it does not conform
to their preferences.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:02.000
And because if it worked,

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.080
my understanding, since it was

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:05.440
demonstrated to be working,

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.720
it was a threat to the way
USAID does its business,

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:12.640
which is to give hundreds of

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:14.160
millions of dollars contracts

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.480
to American based contractors.

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:19.760
And if that program were expanded

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:23.040
and institutionalized
as it should have been,

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:29.360
that will really put USAID's
business model in turmoil.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:34.240
So that is a prime example
of denial of evidence

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:35.600
as you state, by the way,

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.120
that at the time that
program was evaluated,

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.280
it was probably the most rigorously

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:44.240
evaluated large program in global health

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:44.960
around that time.

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:49.040
Now on neo dependency,

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:54.000
let's be upfront about one item,

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:58.160
which is that colonialism
was extremely bad.

00:14:58.160 --> 00:14:59.840
There's no other way to describe it.

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:03.040
Crimes against humanity were

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:04.880
committed by European powers

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.360
and by those who enslaved Africans,

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:11.840
there's no qualifying it, period.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:15.680
And it is also true that
effects of colonialism

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:19.680
are still with us in
terms of the fact that

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:21.600
the colonized societies even though

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.840
they're now politically independent,

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:26.320
the wound endures in terms of their

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:30.000
social policies and the devastation that

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.280
was ricked over the years.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:36.560
Having said that, my
proposition in the book

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:40.000
is that a greater problem,

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:43.040
a problem that is greater
than neocolonialism

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:46.000
and the lasting effect
of colonialism is the,

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.760
what I call neo dependency.

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:52.800
That's the dependency or
the perpetual dependency

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:54.880
of many countries in the

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.040
global south on the global north.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:01.920
And this is manifested clearly,

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:05.200
but not only in the way foreign aid

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:07.840
is mediated by several institutions

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:11.360
and in the way those countries is

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:13.360
I see they really do not want to graduate

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.680
from being dependent on the global north.

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.800
And that if this is going
to be turned around,

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.560
then there needs to be
a fundamental change,

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:27.120
such that those countries become

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:30.720
more reliant on their own revenues,

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:32.560
on their own domestic resources.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:35.840
And that they demonstrate what I will call

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:39.440
increasingly self financed assertiveness.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.480
That really is the core of it.

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:45.360
So while the perennial the bad

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:47.200
effects of colonialism endure,

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:52.320
the central problem as I understand it is

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.080
actually that of neo dependence.

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:57.440
And that leads to several
recommendations in the book.

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:00.560
- Thank you very much Olusoji.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:03.600
And I do want to ask the audience

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:06.880
to please take opportunity of

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:10.320
take this opportunity to
engage with Dr. Olusoji

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:15.920
on some questions and answers based on

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:18.720
these very thought provoking

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:22.160
and important statements that he's making.

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:23.360
So.

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:26.560
- [Tracey] Rafael, we do have
one question that came in.

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:27.360
- Please, please.

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:31.280
- [Tracey] And the question is

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:34.880
how do we as students and staff at Yale

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:36.480
or other powerful institutions

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:39.600
in the global north work advocate in work

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.680
in mitigating the negative effects

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:45.840
of neocolonialism and global health?

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:49.440
- Wow, that's a trillion dollar

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:50.800
question and a great one.

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:54.720
I think before advocating my sense of it

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:58.480
is to be very deeply aware of

00:17:59.200 --> 00:18:00.880
the narrative around us

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:05.200
and how those effects of

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:08.800
neocolonialism modulated in real life.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:14.240
So it starts with the
courses in those schools.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:20.480
My sense is that while epidemiology

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:21.920
and biostatistics

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:23.520
and the behavioral sciences

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:26.480
and health economics remain central

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:30.000
and core to a rigorous curriculum

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.520
in public health, for example.

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:36.160
It is important to pay equally

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:40.160
serious attention to political analysis,

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:44.240
to the political economy of global health,

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:48.240
and to what I will call
influencing policies

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:52.000
and enabling change so that we do not go

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:54.400
into a highly political situation

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:59.680
with purely technocratic tools that assume

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:03.120
that if we just get our regressions right

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:04.560
then everything will fall in place.

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:06.960
That's the first one.

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:11.120
The second step is taking the time

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:14.560
and making a serious effort to understand

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:16.720
the social political narrative

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:18.560
and the social political realities

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:20.560
of the countries in which

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:21.760
we seek to operate.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:23.840
Let me share an example
that is in the book.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.240
In this book, Global Health In Practice.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:31.360
I lived in Ethiopia many years ago

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:34.960
as a young professional working with

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:36.320
the World Health Organization.

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.920
And I was there during the war

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:42.400
that ended in 1991.

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.000
One of the things that I observed

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:48.000
was that many, very
senior technical experts

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:53.280
who had been trained in really high class,

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:56.160
high quality schools of the likes

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:58.720
of Yale and all the others.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.160
They will come into the country

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:05.120
and a number of them will look at

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:06.960
their counterparts and they will see

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:09.440
this stereotypical, poor citizen

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:11.600
of a poor country,

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:13.200
or poor official,

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:15.520
an official of a poor
country that needed help.

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:19.200
And they're promptly proceeded to

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.960
prescribe solutions to those officials.

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:26.640
Now they were doing so with the best

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:29.360
of technical or technocratic intentions,

00:20:29.360 --> 00:20:30.560
if I may use that phrase,

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:35.280
the problem was it did not fit the

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.680
narrative of those officials

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.000
because the officials looked in the mirror

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:43.600
and they saw the descendants of those

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:45.200
who won the battle of Adwa

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:49.920
when the Ethiopians defeated Italians.

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:54.720
And this was no in addition to that,

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.680
they remember the association between the

00:20:57.680 --> 00:20:59.920
Queen of Sheba and King Solomon.

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:02.720
So when they looked in the mirror,

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:04.800
they saw the descendants of

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:06.000
a very proud people.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:09.360
And you can just imagine how these

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:10.960
two narratives collided.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:14.640
And that limited the effectiveness

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:15.600
of those Europeans

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:19.120
and North Americans to whom I referred.

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:22.720
So taking the time to truly understand

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:26.000
the narrative and the
social political dynamics

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.560
of the people with whom
we are engaging is key.

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:31.760
A third one is to be very clear

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:33.920
in terms of what we're optimizing for.

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:38.640
If we're optimizing for
a small pilot project

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:40.960
to demonstrate effect in a population

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:44.000
of 500 people or 5,000 people

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:47.360
in one sub district it's
a legitimate thing to do.

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:55.200
But if our aim is to engage with

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:57.520
and influence what happens all across

00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:01.120
a very large country like Pakistan

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:03.360
or Democratic Republic of Congo,

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:04.720
or all of Nicaragua,

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:07.040
just to say three examples from

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:08.240
different parts of the world,

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:12.560
then we need to understand that we are

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:14.320
engaged in an enterprise that is

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:17.360
substantially political
and proceed as such.

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:22.160
- Thank you very much Olusoji.

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:23.840
And we're doing our best at the

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:25.200
Yale school of Public Health

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:30.240
to actually update or develop new courses

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:34.480
to very strongly emphasize the points

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:38.240
that you just made about how yes

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:43.120
small scale efficacy trials
or studies are important,

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:48.000
but the ability to transfer that crucial

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:50.720
knowledge for the survival

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:52.800
and healthy development of human beings

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:56.080
into large scale sustainable operations

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:57.920
is another ballgame.

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:00.560
And really requires a deep understanding

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:02.640
of the history,

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:03.760
the political,

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:05.760
the economic,

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:07.520
the social systems in the countries.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:12.400
And so I found very rapidly
in Kaduna in Nigeria,

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:14.720
and really understanding how powerful

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:18.640
the community structures are.

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:21.360
And how impossible it is to scale up

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.840
anything if there is no support from

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:29.600
the community leaders for those projects

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:31.280
to end up being implemented

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:33.760
successfully on the ground.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:37.680
So, I really want to urge the audience to

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:41.440
read this very important book

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:43.840
on global health practice

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:47.200
because it really comes from what we,

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:50.320
I like to call a huge global health

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:53.040
practice scholar that has been there,

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:55.120
has done that and has been able to

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:58.400
look at the global health challenges

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:05.280
from many different angles
over several decades.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:09.360
So please keep on sharing with us

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:12.320
your very insightful questions.

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:17.440
So moving on to the next
part of the discussion.

00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:23.360
Olusoji as you know given the context

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:27.520
of the uncertainty with
the COVID 19 pandemic,

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:31.840
the likelihood that it's
not going to be the last

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:36.480
probably at least in my lifetime,

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:40.480
some of our students even our faculty

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:42.480
are a little bit anxious about

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:46.880
how the future jobs in global health

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:48.720
are going to look like.

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:52.480
Which are the skills that the future

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:55.760
global health professionals should have

00:24:55.760 --> 00:25:00.080
to be successful doing their work.

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:03.520
So can you share with us the contributions

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:06.400
from your excellent book towards

00:25:07.840 --> 00:25:13.360
institutions like Yale
to convey to the faculty,

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:15.120
to the students in terms of what

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:18.480
they should be focusing
on to really strive

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:24.560
to become really key global health

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:27.840
practitioners moving forward?

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:29.440
- Thanks Rafael.

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:34.160
I think the probably the single most

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:36.240
important question to ask oneself is

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:38.400
how can I be most useful?

00:25:40.240 --> 00:25:42.720
And that may sound so obvious,

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:47.520
but I think a failure to closely reflect

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:52.000
on that question might lead
to a lot of frustration.

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.440
So if one approaches it from

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:54.880
the point of being useful,

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:58.000
then it means go where the problems are.

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:03.280
And be equipped with the tools to

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:07.200
help better understand those problems

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:13.920
and to help formulate workable solutions

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:17.360
to those problems even if they are not

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:20.880
the ideal solutions from a purely

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:22.720
theoretical point of view.

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.320
So let's compact this.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:28.240
In terms of being useful,

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:36.640
perhaps because of the COVID 19 pandemic

00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:39.760
and the subsequent
attention to global health

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:43.360
for people younger
colleagues who are about to

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:48.080
go into the field I would say you are

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:50.400
actually very fortunate
because you don't have

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:52.960
to explain to anybody
what public health is,

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:55.040
or you don't have to
explain to most people

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:57.520
what public health is or
why it is so important.

00:26:57.520 --> 00:26:59.440
Now everybody gets it, okay.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:04.160
In order to be useful
I think one also needs

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:07.200
to figure out which niche to occupy

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:08.960
or to seek to occupy initially.

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:13.760
So is it to be an analyst helping to

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:16.000
better understand the scale,

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:17.760
the dynamics of health problems.

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:22.480
Is it to be primarily a doer?

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:24.640
And I'm not suggesting that those are

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:26.960
very different, they're intertwined.

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:30.800
In which case it'll make sense to seek

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:33.120
opportunities in entities,

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:36.960
implementing programs in
challenging situations.

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:40.960
And to go with the intention of learning.

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:45.920
Or is it to be primarily
in an advocacy position?

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:47.440
Again, that's not separate from

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:49.760
being an analyst or a doer.

00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:53.280
I'm just trying to simplify
and that's okay too.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:56.960
And then it means understanding whether

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:58.800
one is seeking to be an analyst

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:02.160
in terms of bearing witness,

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:03.920
which has its place,

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:06.880
or whether I want seek to go beyond

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:10.480
bearing witness and nudging,

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:14.720
rich and powerful entities to do better

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:17.760
by the developing countries or nudging

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:19.520
the rulers of developing countries to

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:22.400
do better by their own societies.

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:29.440
One final note on that is overarching

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:30.960
Charles Houston, who was the

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:33.680
Professor of Thurgood Marshall

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:37.760
at the Howard University Law School,

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:39.360
which is just a mile from where

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:41.040
I'm speaking in Washington DC,

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:43.840
had an injunction for his students.

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:49.040
And he said it was not
sufficient to be a lawyer

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:51.360
they also had to be social engineers.

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:55.280
Otherwise they were just being parasites.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:57.760
I think that applies to people in

00:28:57.760 --> 00:28:59.120
global public health, too.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.320
In different ways it's important to have

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:06.640
the mindset of a social engineer

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.560
an analytically rigorous social engineer.

00:29:10.560 --> 00:29:15.520
Otherwise one might
unwittingly become a parasite.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:19.120
So in some, the future is bright

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:21.200
for those entering global health.

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:24.560
There are just so many challenges

00:29:24.560 --> 00:29:26.320
waiting to be tackled

00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:30.080
and please go forth and be useful.

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:33.600
- Thank you so very much for

00:29:33.600 --> 00:29:36.160
such an inspiring answer.

00:29:36.880 --> 00:29:43.280
And Abel Galetta is also asking,

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:47.600
given the complex skills essentially

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:50.720
that are needed here to succeed

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:53.840
in the field of global health.

00:29:54.880 --> 00:29:57.280
How do you overcome the challenges

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:01.840
that inevitably you are going to find

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:04.240
as an early career professional?

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:07.840
What did you do to overcome
those challenge yourself?

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:10.960
What words of wisdom your book

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:14.640
has to offer to Abel and colleagues?

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:19.680
- I really, I really
appreciate your question.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:24.240
This is Abel Galetta if I'm
looking at the chat box.

00:30:24.240 --> 00:30:24.800
Great question.

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:29.120
I think it's important
to have good mentors

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:30.720
because when you're starting out

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:34.400
there are many things that are just so new

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:36.080
and they can be confusing,

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:38.400
and sometimes you might struggle with

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:40.880
how to prioritize and what to filter out.

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:44.800
So having a mentor or a set of mentors

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:46.480
in my view is really important.

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:54.240
A second one is approaching global health

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:58.720
or the mindset that every obstacle is an

00:30:58.720 --> 00:31:01.840
opportunity to learn
and get better and grow.

00:31:01.840 --> 00:31:09.920
I think those two are
really they are fundamental.

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:11.360
Yeah, those are the two key ones

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:15.040
that I would say early on finding,

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:17.840
having good mentors and
approaching obstacles

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:20.880
from the perspective of learning.

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:22.640
I think those are really,
those are critical.

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:25.280
- Yes, thank you Olusoji very much.

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:27.520
And then linking this somewhat to

00:31:27.520 --> 00:31:30.240
the previous statement that you made about

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:33.120
the US Agency of
International Development,

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:35.600
which is, as we know
is not unique to USAID

00:31:36.880 --> 00:31:39.520
it's a very common situation with

00:31:40.560 --> 00:31:45.040
international aid
agencies and the fact that

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:46.240
they all have their agendas,

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:51.840
their priorities, and sometimes
from almost overnight,

00:31:51.840 --> 00:31:55.120
they change and countries that are

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:56.960
being affected by huge problems

00:31:56.960 --> 00:31:58.560
and working with them for decades,

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:01.040
do not have a say on when and how

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:05.200
those pivot points happen.

00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:11.520
So several of my students
have asked me in the past

00:32:12.960 --> 00:32:15.440
is there any hope that I can actually work

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:17.040
for an agency like that and change it

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:18.560
from the inside out?

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:22.400
If I choose not to work in an agency

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:24.800
like that then where am I going to work?

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:28.080
Where am I going to be
able to make a difference?

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:31.120
And I get a very well your important point

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:35.840
about advocacy and
grassroots work and so on,

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:39.120
but also in the context that

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:42.560
financial resources are needed as well

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:49.040
to engage in large scale
programming and so on.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:53.200
And what would you advise our students

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:56.080
regarding the sense of hopelessness that

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:58.480
some of them have that it's very

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:00.560
difficult would be almost impossible to

00:33:00.560 --> 00:33:06.320
change the way the key
agencies are governed,

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:08.080
the structures that they have

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:13.040
and somewhat arbitrary way in which that

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:14.560
they follow to make decision.

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:20.160
- Yeah, Rafael I would say they

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:23.840
are very complex institutions.

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:27.760
And within that complexities there are

00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:31.680
opportunities for
professionals who are rigorous,

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:35.040
who are discerning and who are committed

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:36.320
to making a difference.

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:41.280
So even for younger members or staff,

00:33:43.520 --> 00:33:46.960
it means being very credible in ones

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:51.040
own analytical work and developing a way

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:53.840
of engaging with more senior people in the

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:58.400
institutions to inform their thinking

00:33:59.600 --> 00:34:03.280
and to change the way
they look at problems.

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:07.520
And if that change means a 25% improvement

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:09.760
over what the world have done before,

00:34:10.640 --> 00:34:12.640
that's a win to start with.

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:18.720
But at that stage for the
entry level professionals,

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:20.640
it means their span of control

00:34:20.640 --> 00:34:22.480
is relatively narrow, that's true.

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:25.760
But over time you get a much broader

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:29.360
and increasingly a bigger span of control.

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:34.160
And therefore the scope
to influence decisions

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:37.200
and influence policies
becomes even larger.

00:34:40.160 --> 00:34:43.520
So I can share a few examples with you.

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:45.760
There's a chapter on Russia in the book.

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:52.480
And yes, I led the team
that did that turnaround.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:56.160
I also had a vice president at the time

00:34:56.160 --> 00:35:00.160
for the region who was supportive

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:01.840
of the effort that we made.

00:35:03.920 --> 00:35:06.960
So that's a positive aspect.

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:10.240
And there were several other aspects

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:12.800
in which I was involved,

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:17.200
both analytical work and
program implementation

00:35:17.200 --> 00:35:19.040
and financing, et cetera.

00:35:19.040 --> 00:35:22.960
So it is not a case of black or white that

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:27.200
institutions are absolutely ill deemable.

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:30.720
I think it's the case
that they're very complex.

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:35.120
And within that complexity
there are opportunities

00:35:35.120 --> 00:35:36.720
for public health professionals to

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:38.240
make a serious difference.

00:35:39.040 --> 00:35:44.160
Having said that in terms
of the macro system,

00:35:44.160 --> 00:35:47.680
realigning the macro system,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:50.480
Well, that is beyond the purview of any

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:53.760
particular individual in global health

00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:57.280
or agriculture or transportation,

00:35:57.280 --> 00:36:00.000
whichever sector you primarily work in.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:04.800
That becomes a political question

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:09.120
in terms of the global
financial architecture.

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:12.080
And that is part of the point in the book.

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:13.840
So please, if you are wondering,

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:16.480
is it a hopeless task to work with

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:19.920
or in those large agencies, like WHO,

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:22.560
the World Bank, et cetera, et cetera.

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:23.280
No, it is not.

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:24.880
There are vast opportunities there

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:26.640
and I will seriously encourage you

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:28.840
to seize those opportunities.

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:31.840
- Thank you very much Olusoji.

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:33.120
And for the audience, please,

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:36.160
if you want to share with Olusoji

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:40.160
any personal experiences working for

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:43.680
agencies or in the field,

00:36:43.680 --> 00:36:46.320
your academic institutions and so on,

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:48.000
and what have you learned from them

00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:50.880
we would very much welcome

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:54.560
your sharing of those experiences.

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:59.440
So I want to move on now towards

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:01.280
the last phase of our conversation,

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:08.800
which is related to how
to maintain initiatives,

00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:10.320
how to maintain traction,

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:17.680
how to ensure that the
relatively few programs

00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:20.640
that have been successfully scaled up

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:25.840
across countries essentially keep on going

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:30.880
and they don't fall back to the

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:35.440
chaos that was there before they emerged.

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:38.000
So in terms of sustainability of programs,

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:41.120
what lessons does your work offers to us?

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:45.520
- A great question.

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:48.240
And perhaps is the perennial question.

00:37:49.360 --> 00:37:54.160
In my experience, I think programs become

00:37:54.160 --> 00:37:57.120
sustainable on multiple dimensions.

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:00.720
Quite often when we talk
about sustainability,

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:04.560
we're talking about
financial sustainability

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:06.240
and technical sustainability,

00:38:06.240 --> 00:38:08.480
both of which are highly important.

00:38:10.640 --> 00:38:12.160
There is another dimension,

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:14.400
which is political sustainability.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:17.840
And for the median leader,

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:24.000
a program is sustainable
when the perceived

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:26.240
political benefits of that program

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:28.640
of continuing that program

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:31.840
or enlarging it when they outweigh

00:38:33.280 --> 00:38:35.920
the political risks of not continuing it.

00:38:37.520 --> 00:38:41.200
Which is why it is very important for

00:38:41.200 --> 00:38:46.000
the political leaders in societies to

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:48.720
be directly engaged in and to own

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:51.520
large programs in their own countries.

00:38:52.960 --> 00:38:57.520
It's also very important for the citizens

00:38:57.520 --> 00:39:00.960
of those societies to see and feel

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:02.720
the benefits of those programs.

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:06.640
If the benefits of a program are obscure

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:09.280
well, there's unlikely to be much

00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:10.880
social mobilization behind it,

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:14.880
but if they're visible they're actually

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:16.560
communicated to the public

00:39:17.360 --> 00:39:21.600
and the successive government know that

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:24.000
these programs are actually
delivering good things.

00:39:25.360 --> 00:39:27.120
Then they're more likely to at least

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:29.680
make an effort to continue those programs.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:32.240
There's another there's a little dimension

00:39:32.240 --> 00:39:34.400
that I would like to
draw our attention to.

00:39:34.400 --> 00:39:37.040
And this is the compared to what question.

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:42.400
So if you engage in a large program

00:39:43.280 --> 00:39:45.600
and just for discussion sake,

00:39:46.240 --> 00:39:48.000
let's just say the success rate before

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:50.960
you went in was 10%

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:57.360
and you are able to take it to 60%.

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:00.400
Obviously that's not perfect, okay.

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:02.640
You really want to hit
something much higher.

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:06.160
All of that things being equal.

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:09.920
I think it is wise to celebrate

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:12.400
that improvement from 10 to 60%.

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:17.040
And then seek to take it to 70%.

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:20.960
But sometimes in global public health,

00:40:20.960 --> 00:40:23.360
I have seen situations in which if

00:40:23.360 --> 00:40:27.360
it cannot go from 10% to a 100%

00:40:29.120 --> 00:40:31.440
then you have many advocates saying,

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:33.120
"Okay, it's a failure, it's bad."

00:40:35.200 --> 00:40:36.800
So it's a binary situation,

00:40:37.760 --> 00:40:39.760
which is just so self-defeating.

00:40:39.760 --> 00:40:43.120
So again, for all of us
going into this field

00:40:43.120 --> 00:40:44.000
or those who are there

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:46.560
and seeking to make
even more improvements,

00:40:47.200 --> 00:40:48.400
let's keep this in mind.

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:52.000
The compared to what question,

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:55.920
because quite often the counterfactual

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:59.200
is not utopian perfection.

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:02.320
The counterfactual is exactly something

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:04.160
that is a really bad situation.

00:41:04.720 --> 00:41:07.680
So if it's getting better celebrate it,

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:08.800
don't be complacent.

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:09.840
Don't be satisfied.

00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:11.680
Celebrate that success.

00:41:11.680 --> 00:41:13.040
And just keep pushing ahead.

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:15.440
Don't just throw it all away

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:18.920
because you cannot achieve
immediate perfection.

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:21.680
- Absolutely Olusoji couldn't,

00:41:21.680 --> 00:41:23.360
well, I say absolutely.

00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:24.728
(speaker laughing)

00:41:24.728 --> 00:41:26.160
I myself, my experience

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:27.280
I couldn't agree more

00:41:27.280 --> 00:41:29.680
because I really think that

00:41:29.680 --> 00:41:31.760
that goes back to your point of the

00:41:31.760 --> 00:41:34.240
importance of understanding that

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:39.120
complex causes behind these problems.

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:43.520
Many of which have been
with us for centuries.

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:46.480
And that we need to be realistic as to

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:49.360
what is a temporary solution.

00:41:49.360 --> 00:41:52.080
What is a short term technical solution?

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:54.080
What at the end of the day would be

00:41:54.080 --> 00:41:57.760
a real long term solution.

00:41:57.760 --> 00:42:02.560
So if everybody's contributing
in a coordinated way,

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:06.560
I think with their 10 cents, so to speak,

00:42:06.560 --> 00:42:09.680
to improve them it reaches a point where

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:12.640
those improvements are not attributed

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:15.840
and cannot be discounted.

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:21.520
So Pranuv Savanur has a very

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:23.840
thoughtful question for you.

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:27.120
So how can we build a sustained,

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:30.320
a non partisan political
will and commitment

00:42:30.320 --> 00:42:32.000
for global health financing.

00:42:32.560 --> 00:42:38.320
And he has in mind the
upcoming funding renewals

00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:40.480
for programs like the global fund Gavi,

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:43.280
the World Health Organization

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:49.280
and so on how to separate politics from

00:42:49.280 --> 00:42:50.960
the true mission of global health.

00:42:50.960 --> 00:42:52.800
As a way of understand this question.

00:42:53.640 --> 00:42:57.840
- Yeah, great question Pranuv.

00:42:59.200 --> 00:43:02.560
First, politics cannot
be separated from it.

00:43:04.560 --> 00:43:06.400
I think what can probably be

00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:08.960
separated from it or what we should make

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:12.960
an effort to separate
from it is toxic politics.

00:43:15.840 --> 00:43:19.360
Now, on the substance of the question,

00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:22.400
I think for entities like Gavi

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:27.200
and the Global Fund the
real challenge as I see it

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:31.120
is how they will replenish to transition

00:43:31.120 --> 00:43:33.760
and exit from what they're currently doing

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:35.520
in the way they're currently doing it.

00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:39.840
So what will it take, for example,

00:43:40.400 --> 00:43:42.480
such that eight years from now

00:43:42.480 --> 00:43:44.160
seeing the year 2030,

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:49.920
the countries themselves are

00:43:49.920 --> 00:43:52.560
the dominance financiers of those

00:43:53.200 --> 00:43:55.360
basic health commodities

00:43:55.360 --> 00:43:57.600
and of those basic health services

00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:01.600
that Gavi and the Global Fund

00:44:01.600 --> 00:44:03.680
disproportionately financed today.

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:07.520
So if you take a specific,

00:44:07.520 --> 00:44:09.280
just take a specific example,

00:44:09.280 --> 00:44:11.040
let's just zero in on malaria.

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:17.200
Last year, WHO recommended widespread use

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:19.360
of the RTS,S malaria vaccine.

00:44:19.360 --> 00:44:20.480
The first time it's done that for

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:22.480
a malaria vaccine on a large scale.

00:44:24.080 --> 00:44:26.000
And normally one will expect that

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:28.400
since this disease has been around for

00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:30.320
millennia and is such a big problem,

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:35.840
the leaders of the countries will lead

00:44:37.040 --> 00:44:39.760
and commit to funding the purchase of

00:44:39.760 --> 00:44:42.720
those vaccines
predominantly by themselves.

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:47.520
Instead, what happened
was that Gavi announced

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:48.960
that it was going to commit,

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:52.000
I think about 156 million over the

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:54.000
next few years to those vaccines.

00:44:55.280 --> 00:45:00.240
Now for is a child who
gets that vaccine in Zambia

00:45:00.240 --> 00:45:02.080
or Liberia or wherever,

00:45:02.080 --> 00:45:03.600
is that a bad thing?

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:05.280
No, it's a good thing.

00:45:06.400 --> 00:45:07.920
The problem is the following,

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:11.920
Gavi was substituting itself again

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:16.560
for the leadership domestic leadership

00:45:16.560 --> 00:45:17.840
in those countries.

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:21.920
And that was very poor judgment

00:45:21.920 --> 00:45:25.600
on the part of Gavi, okay.

00:45:28.160 --> 00:45:29.280
Still on malaria.

00:45:30.640 --> 00:45:32.160
The very last estimate from the

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:36.560
World Malaria Report
published by WHO indicate

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:41.200
that two thirds of the expenditures

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:42.880
of malaria control and elimination

00:45:43.600 --> 00:45:45.120
come from external sources.

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:47.120
That's two thirds, 67%.

00:45:49.280 --> 00:45:53.280
So this needs to change cannot have

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:58.400
a situation where permanently there is

00:45:58.400 --> 00:46:01.760
dependency on external financiers.

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:03.680
So for replenishment,

00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:06.000
it should be replenishment for transition.

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:08.640
Not replenishment for self perpetuation.

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:12.000
That's the real key here that is

00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:13.040
that I really want to,

00:46:13.040 --> 00:46:14.800
that I really wanna have on.

00:46:14.800 --> 00:46:19.040
So that applies to Global Fund and Gavi.

00:46:19.920 --> 00:46:23.600
As for WHO I really am not convinced

00:46:23.600 --> 00:46:26.240
that a replenishment model is

00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:28.400
the right way to finance WHO

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:33.040
is the central health leadership agency,

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:35.200
the technical normative agency as well.

00:46:36.240 --> 00:46:39.040
And it seems to me that having that

00:46:39.040 --> 00:46:41.840
core financing sorted out is key.

00:46:43.600 --> 00:46:45.920
I don't think that also means WHO

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:48.400
should be responsible
for everything or try

00:46:48.400 --> 00:46:50.720
to do everything under the sun in health.

00:46:51.840 --> 00:46:53.920
It does not mean WHO has to attend

00:46:53.920 --> 00:46:55.120
every single meeting in

00:46:55.120 --> 00:46:58.320
every single village on health.

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:04.880
So there's a case to be made for

00:47:04.880 --> 00:47:10.000
concentrating the effort on WHO

00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:15.120
on some core functions
and for funding them well

00:47:15.120 --> 00:47:17.280
from through assess contribution to

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:20.560
the regular budget not
extra budgetary allocation.

00:47:21.840 --> 00:47:22.800
Back to you Rafael.

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:26.320
- Yes, wow I think we could stay here for

00:47:26.320 --> 00:47:30.400
three hours speaking with you.

00:47:30.400 --> 00:47:35.360
What an engaging and
thoughtful conversation.

00:47:35.360 --> 00:47:37.600
And I feel like really everyone

00:47:37.600 --> 00:47:40.560
should read Olusoji's book.

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:47.200
Not many people have the
very, very holistic experience

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:52.240
that he has in global health.

00:47:52.240 --> 00:47:56.720
Sumaira Barsada wants to thank you

00:47:56.720 --> 00:48:00.080
very much for your talk.

00:48:00.080 --> 00:48:02.960
She finds it very inspiring.

00:48:02.960 --> 00:48:05.200
She just went to Amazon
and order your book

00:48:05.200 --> 00:48:10.080
so that you know and she
can't wait to read it.

00:48:10.080 --> 00:48:14.960
So I'm very, very glad
Sumaira that you got inspired

00:48:16.160 --> 00:48:18.640
to do that during this conversation.

00:48:18.640 --> 00:48:21.120
And I'm sure Olusoji will be very happy

00:48:21.120 --> 00:48:23.120
to send your book when you get it.

00:48:23.120 --> 00:48:25.680
We can easily arranged for that I think.

00:48:27.120 --> 00:48:34.960
So, Olusoji to wrap up the
conversation on my end.

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:39.680
I really get your very
central message here.

00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:40.640
It's loud and clear.

00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:46.160
Unless governments end
up being empowered to own

00:48:47.520 --> 00:48:52.320
the systems, the structures,

00:48:54.080 --> 00:48:57.760
the infrastructure that
is needed to improve

00:48:59.440 --> 00:49:02.400
public health within their countries

00:49:02.400 --> 00:49:04.160
and to do so with equity

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:05.920
following human rights principles,

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:07.680
social justice principles,

00:49:07.680 --> 00:49:12.480
and so on is very
difficult to actually see

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:18.400
how improvement can be
made on a global level.

00:49:19.520 --> 00:49:22.720
We know that oftentimes there is this

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:25.520
tension between governments feeling

00:49:25.520 --> 00:49:27.680
oh NGOs know how to do it,

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:29.440
they're really great at doing it

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:30.240
have them do it.

00:49:31.440 --> 00:49:34.800
NGOs saying, we cannot do
this on a national scale.

00:49:34.800 --> 00:49:37.120
We cannot keep this sustainable.

00:49:37.120 --> 00:49:41.440
And oftentimes to me becomes
an absurd either or argument.

00:49:41.440 --> 00:49:43.680
I really think that there is a

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:48.080
very special place for NGOs as part of

00:49:48.080 --> 00:49:51.520
a new global health governance system.

00:49:51.520 --> 00:49:54.480
But at the same time, there is absolutely

00:49:54.480 --> 00:49:57.280
no reason why they cannot be part of

00:49:57.280 --> 00:50:00.480
a government strategy
when the government's own

00:50:04.800 --> 00:50:06.640
the programs, the policies

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:09.040
and so on to keep on working together.

00:50:09.040 --> 00:50:12.800
So I mean, do you agree with that or not?

00:50:13.360 --> 00:50:15.520
And what can be done to improve that?

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:19.760
A very, very interesting dynamic that

00:50:19.760 --> 00:50:23.760
I think needs to be addressed to improve

00:50:23.760 --> 00:50:26.480
much more public health across the globe.

00:50:28.040 --> 00:50:32.080
- Yeah, Rafael I think you
touch on an important point.

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:40.880
There is no viable
substitute in the medium term

00:50:40.880 --> 00:50:46.560
for a responsible government
that leads its own,

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:48.960
that leads policies within its own country

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:53.840
and walks across sectors
within its own country.

00:50:55.520 --> 00:50:57.520
And that includes financing of course.

00:50:58.400 --> 00:51:00.000
But there is a difference between

00:51:01.120 --> 00:51:03.680
public financing on the one hand

00:51:04.320 --> 00:51:07.600
and public delivery on the other hand.

00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:09.120
So it's entirely possible.

00:51:09.120 --> 00:51:10.880
Most systems are mixed, anyway.

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:16.080
It's entirely possible for most of

00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:17.760
the expenditure in a country,

00:51:17.760 --> 00:51:19.120
Most of the health expenditure in

00:51:19.120 --> 00:51:22.560
a country to be to be publicly financed,

00:51:23.360 --> 00:51:25.360
but for services to be delivered by

00:51:25.360 --> 00:51:27.680
a combination of public facilities,

00:51:28.560 --> 00:51:31.920
not for profit NGO
facilities, for example.

00:51:31.920 --> 00:51:34.480
And for profit facilities who might be

00:51:34.480 --> 00:51:38.160
contracted by government
to deliver those services

00:51:38.160 --> 00:51:40.560
or contracted by a health insurance agency

00:51:40.560 --> 00:51:41.840
to deliver those services.

00:51:43.040 --> 00:51:46.800
And there is a serious
role for civil society

00:51:46.800 --> 00:51:49.840
to play in that the NGOs and the CSOs,

00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:53.920
if one wants to delineate both in terms of

00:51:55.040 --> 00:51:56.560
keeping government honest,

00:51:56.560 --> 00:51:57.920
if I may use that expression,

00:51:58.960 --> 00:52:01.840
mobilizing the society
and shining the light

00:52:01.840 --> 00:52:03.840
in otherwise dark areas.

00:52:04.560 --> 00:52:08.960
But the NGOs and CFOs in my understanding

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:16.640
do not substitute for those
core governance policy

00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:20.000
and policy translation functions that can

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:21.600
only be done by government.

00:52:21.600 --> 00:52:23.280
And I will even go
further and say one thing.

00:52:25.520 --> 00:52:28.720
In my experience both
the country based NGOs

00:52:28.720 --> 00:52:30.480
and the international NGOs in

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:32.800
different places have made really

00:52:32.800 --> 00:52:34.800
serious contributions to
health service delivery,

00:52:34.800 --> 00:52:36.640
including in some very
hard to reach places.

00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:42.240
At the same time it is the case that

00:52:42.240 --> 00:52:44.000
some of the northern NGOs,

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:45.840
not all of them,

00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:51.120
some of them sometimes unwittingly perhaps

00:52:51.120 --> 00:52:53.280
substitute try to substitute themselves

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:54.720
for the government of those countries.

00:52:56.240 --> 00:52:58.960
So they don't like
international organizations

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:01.760
to dictate policies to countries.

00:53:01.760 --> 00:53:02.800
And they're right.

00:53:02.800 --> 00:53:04.320
International organizations should not

00:53:04.320 --> 00:53:06.000
dictate policies to the countries.

00:53:06.560 --> 00:53:09.360
But in my experience,
quite a few of those NGOs

00:53:09.920 --> 00:53:12.640
will like international organizations to

00:53:12.640 --> 00:53:15.440
dictate the NGOs preferences
to the countries.

00:53:17.120 --> 00:53:18.240
And that needs to stop.

00:53:19.600 --> 00:53:23.280
In addition to that, quite a few of those

00:53:23.280 --> 00:53:25.840
international organizations
do the countries

00:53:25.840 --> 00:53:29.360
a disservice when they blame international

00:53:29.360 --> 00:53:31.440
financing institutions for everything

00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:36.560
because that infantalizes the governments

00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:38.960
of those low and lower
middle income countries.

00:53:39.680 --> 00:53:42.640
It's as if the governments
cannot think for themselves,

00:53:42.640 --> 00:53:44.400
they cannot make decisions for themselves.

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:46.560
And it becomes very convenient to

00:53:46.560 --> 00:53:48.800
just blame everything on outsiders.

00:53:50.240 --> 00:53:55.200
So in that regard I think quite a few of

00:53:55.200 --> 00:54:00.320
the large northern NGOs need
to do some introspection.

00:54:02.560 --> 00:54:06.160
And avoid a situation
in which they position

00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:08.480
themselves as if they are the legitimate

00:54:08.480 --> 00:54:11.520
representatives of large populations,

00:54:11.520 --> 00:54:13.360
those light populations in low

00:54:13.360 --> 00:54:15.280
and lower middle income countries.

00:54:15.280 --> 00:54:16.160
They're not.

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:17.920
And they cannot substitute
for those people.

00:54:20.240 --> 00:54:23.520
I also wanna thank our colleagues who have

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:27.680
sent text messages, I think Shidrak also,

00:54:27.680 --> 00:54:28.880
thanks for getting the book.

00:54:28.880 --> 00:54:30.800
And I look forward to
engaging with you all.

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:33.360
Please feel free to join me on Twitter.

00:54:33.360 --> 00:54:34.819
Back to you Rafael.

00:54:34.819 --> 00:54:37.280
- And if I can squeeze
in one last question.

00:54:37.280 --> 00:54:38.076
- [Olusoji] Please.

00:54:38.076 --> 00:54:39.840
- And I apologize for those that

00:54:39.840 --> 00:54:41.280
I cannot ask your questions.

00:54:41.840 --> 00:54:43.920
But I think this is a
wonderful one to wrap

00:54:43.920 --> 00:54:45.040
this conversation up.

00:54:45.040 --> 00:54:47.920
And it comes from Avicii Ijasa

00:54:47.920 --> 00:54:50.720
and it is about what does a future hold

00:54:50.720 --> 00:54:52.800
for healthcare systems.

00:54:54.720 --> 00:54:58.480
At the end of the day,
he asks if future states

00:54:59.280 --> 00:55:02.000
in Sub-Saharan Africa currently almost

00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:04.800
no country meets the World
Bank and WHO benchmarks

00:55:04.800 --> 00:55:07.440
for universal health coverage

00:55:07.440 --> 00:55:12.080
following the SDG 2030.

00:55:12.080 --> 00:55:14.720
And in that context of
what has just happened

00:55:14.720 --> 00:55:16.720
or is happening with COVID 19,

00:55:18.880 --> 00:55:21.120
what is the future for healthcare systems

00:55:21.120 --> 00:55:23.640
strengthening or for that agenda?

00:55:23.640 --> 00:55:26.800
- It's a great question
on which to wrap up.

00:55:26.800 --> 00:55:27.920
I agree with you.

00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:30.640
I think the COVID 19 has been a clarifier.

00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:32.320
Or it is the clarifier.

00:55:32.320 --> 00:55:35.040
It's not yet over and it's brought a lot

00:55:35.040 --> 00:55:36.640
of stress on societies.

00:55:37.920 --> 00:55:41.200
On the positive side
it has also made clear

00:55:41.200 --> 00:55:43.920
the importance of self reliance by

00:55:43.920 --> 00:55:45.680
countries of the global south

00:55:46.880 --> 00:55:48.560
because what has happened during COVID

00:55:48.560 --> 00:55:50.320
is you realize when COVID struck,

00:55:51.280 --> 00:55:51.920
let's face it,

00:55:52.480 --> 00:55:54.400
the message from the
global north was look,

00:55:54.400 --> 00:55:55.200
you are on your own.

00:55:56.480 --> 00:55:57.600
So what does this mean?

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:00.880
It means that global south
needs to face its reality

00:56:00.880 --> 00:56:02.880
when it comes to
universal health coverage,

00:56:02.880 --> 00:56:04.320
Nobody is going to,

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:07.680
nobody from outside is
gonna solve it for them.

00:56:09.040 --> 00:56:13.600
So my mind, the way to approach this is

00:56:13.600 --> 00:56:15.680
what has already been described as

00:56:15.680 --> 00:56:19.360
progressive universalism start with

00:56:19.360 --> 00:56:23.280
an emphasis on a core
of essential services,

00:56:23.840 --> 00:56:25.120
what some people refer to as

00:56:25.120 --> 00:56:26.720
essential package of services

00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:28.480
or essential health package.

00:56:29.360 --> 00:56:31.760
And try to get this to
the whole population.

00:56:32.400 --> 00:56:36.240
I think that is really key
in all our conversations.

00:56:36.240 --> 00:56:37.040
So that's number one.

00:56:37.760 --> 00:56:40.640
A second pillar of this in terms of

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:42.640
what of the how to finance it

00:56:43.520 --> 00:56:45.520
is to move away from systems

00:56:45.520 --> 00:56:46.640
in which many countries,

00:56:48.160 --> 00:56:50.560
a large proportion of financing comes from

00:56:51.920 --> 00:56:54.080
private out of pocket expenditures,

00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:56.240
which is very regressive.

00:56:56.240 --> 00:56:57.440
It's not equitable.

00:56:57.440 --> 00:56:58.880
It causes financial hardship.

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:05.280
And to move more towards
publicly financed health systems.

00:57:06.080 --> 00:57:08.000
But that also means doing better

00:57:08.000 --> 00:57:10.320
in terms of domestic tax collection.

00:57:11.040 --> 00:57:13.280
Many of the countries do very poorly

00:57:13.280 --> 00:57:15.120
in terms of collecting their own taxes.

00:57:16.720 --> 00:57:18.240
This are two of the pillars.

00:57:18.240 --> 00:57:19.200
Not all, not all.

00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:20.880
These are two of the pillars required to

00:57:20.880 --> 00:57:23.840
make progress towards
universal health coverage.

00:57:23.840 --> 00:57:25.280
There's the paper that I wrote about

00:57:25.280 --> 00:57:27.600
four years ago with some colleagues,

00:57:27.600 --> 00:57:31.200
we called it, The Economic
Transition of Health.

00:57:31.200 --> 00:57:33.280
And I think we tried to unpack in that

00:57:33.280 --> 00:57:35.600
we anticipated some of these

00:57:35.600 --> 00:57:38.160
constraints that have now arisen.

00:57:38.160 --> 00:57:39.840
Great question, Avicii

00:57:39.840 --> 00:57:44.080
- Well, on that note, please join me in

00:57:44.080 --> 00:57:48.480
a warmly thanking
Olusoji for the wonderful

00:57:48.480 --> 00:57:50.880
contribution he has
made through his career

00:57:50.880 --> 00:57:54.800
crystallized in his book
and for agreeing to have

00:57:54.800 --> 00:57:57.680
this wonderful conversation with us today.

00:57:57.680 --> 00:57:59.520
Thank you very, very much Olusoji.

00:57:59.520 --> 00:58:01.032
(speaker clapping)

00:58:01.032 --> 00:58:02.480
- Thank you, Raphael and colleagues.

00:58:02.480 --> 00:58:03.360
Thanks everybody.

00:58:03.360 --> 00:58:05.440
I really appreciate this
discussion with you.

00:58:05.440 --> 00:58:05.840
Thank you.

00:58:05.840 --> 00:58:07.840
- [Tracey] Thank you.

